/**
  @page guides-thread-safety Thread safety and parallelism
  @brief Object ownership, synchronization and parallel assembly.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-threads-model The thread-safety model

  Rodin parallelizes mesh iteration within its assembly backends. Each worker
  evaluates local forms using its own expression objects.

  - **Per-object serial access.** Every variational node derives from
    @ref Rodin::FormLanguage::Base "FormLanguage::Base". Methods of a given
    integrator, function, shape function or problem are called by one thread
    at a time.
  - **Per-thread expression trees.** Expressions deep-copy their operands
    through `copy()`. Parallel evaluation uses a separate integrator or
    function instance for each worker.
  - **Concurrent immutable reads.** Fully constructed meshes, connectivity
    and finite element spaces support concurrent reads. Access to lazily
    computed state follows the synchronization contract of the owning class.
  - **Exclusive mutation.** Coordinate updates, transformation attachment,
    connectivity computation, mesh builders and grid-function writes require
    external synchronization. Required incidence relations are computed
    before entering a parallel region.

  @section guides-threads-module Synchronization of shared state

  The class owning shared state defines its synchronization contract.
  @ref Rodin::Geometry::AttributeIndex "AttributeIndex", for example, uses
  atomic attribute slots and per-dimension mutexes for storage growth and
  writes. Its contract specifies concurrent slot access and the structural
  operations requiring external synchronization. Mesh and field mutations
  follow the contracts of their respective classes.

  @section guides-threads-assembly Parallel assembly

  The @ref Rodin::Assembly "Assembly" module provides sequential and OpenMP
  backends, selected by <a href="_assembly_2_default_8h.html">Assembly/Default.h</a>
  according to the build configuration. During OpenMP evaluation, each worker
  processes a subset of polytopes and stores local contributions. The results
  are then merged into the global matrix and vector.

  Floating-point summation order can vary with thread count. Numerical
  comparisons therefore use the specified solver and regression tolerances.

  Assembly performance depends on memory bandwidth and work per quadrature
  point. Record thread counts, hardware and complete workload details when
  evaluating scaling. Reusing
  cell- and quadrature-dependent quantities in an integrator's bind step
  reduces evaluation work. Thread scaling is measured for the target workload.

  MPI-distributed assembly is provided by the mirrored `MPI/` module tree and
  composes with local assembly; see @ref guides-mpi.

  @section guides-threads-petsc External libraries

  - **PETSc.** Rodin calls PETSc from one thread per rank. For PETSc-dominated
    runs, `OMP_NUM_THREADS=1` gives a serial local execution path while MPI
    distributes the work. Mixed OpenMP/PETSc programs must satisfy PETSc's
    per-object access requirements.
  - **Eigen.** Const operations support concurrent access. Eigen's internal
    OpenMP products can nest within Rodin's parallel regions; OpenMP nesting
    controls limit oversubscription.

  @section guides-threads-writing Writing parallel-friendly code

  A mesh-sweeping algorithm separates a read-only evaluation phase from a
  deterministic commit phase. Evaluation produces independent proposals over
  polytopes. Commit applies a compatible subset in a defined order. This
  organization supports sequential and parallel execution with the same
  operation semantics.

  @section guides-threads-seealso See also

  - @ref guides-mpi — distributed meshes and assembly
  - @ref guides-petsc — PETSc-backed solves
  - @ref Rodin::Assembly "Assembly" — backend selection
*/
